      ":question/math-academy-id": "q-240647",
      ":question/problem": "![](images/d6/d6b4ea66392a4955e9c1f66e4751f3e3c1545fbff020c4279c28f7e6d87bcd0f.png)\n\nIn the diagram above, the angles $m\u2220PQR + m\u2220UTW = 180^{\u2218}$.\n\nConsider the following statement:\n\n> *The lines $RS^(\u27f7)$ and $UV^(\u27f7)$ are parallel.*\n\nThe table below gives the proof of the statement. Select the correct options in the table and thus complete the proof.\n\n|  | Statement | Reason |\n| --- | ---: | ---: |\n| 1 | $m\u2220PQR + m\u2220UTW = 180^{\u2218}$ | Given |\n| 2 | $m\u2220PQR = 180^{\u2218} - m\u2220UTW$ | Addition principle |\n| 3 | $\u2220UTW$ and $\u2220UTQ$ are supplementary | {{field-1}} |\n| 4 | $m\u2220UTW + m\u2220UTQ = 180^{\u2218}$ | Definition of supplementary angles |\n| 5 | $m\u2220UTQ = 180^{\u2218} - m\u2220UTW$ | Addition principle |\n| 6 | $m\u2220UTQ = m\u2220PQR$ | Substitution |\n| 7 | $\u2220UTQ\u2245\u2220PQR$ | {{field-2}} |\n| 8 | $RS^(\u27f7)$ and $UV^(\u27f7)$ are parallel lines | {{field-3}} |"
    }
